# Cloudflare DNS Migration - thukool.online

## Overview
Migrated DNS management from Namecheap to Cloudflare for thukool.online, 
a domain hosting two live Node.js backends on AWS EC2.

## Architecture
Before: Visitor → Namecheap DNS → EC2 (direct)
After: Visitor → Cloudflare DNS → EC2 (direct, grey cloud for backends)

## What Was Done
- Audited existing DNS records (A, MX, TXT, SPF, DKIM, DMARC)
- Manually added missing subdomain records (haven, secret) not detected in scan
- Preserved Zoho email records and Resend DKIM records
- Configured SSL/TLS mode to Full (strict) - compatible with existing Let's Encrypt certs
- Set backend subdomains to DNS only (grey cloud) to prevent API disruption
- Changed nameservers from Namecheap to Cloudflare

## Key Decisions
- Backends kept DNS-only: Cloudflare's bot challenge system would break programmatic API requests
- Full (strict) SSL: prevents redirect loops with existing Let's Encrypt certificates
- All email records migrated before nameserver change to prevent mail disruption

## Tools Used
- Cloudflare Free Plan
- Namecheap (registrar)
- AWS EC2 (origin server)
- Let's Encrypt / Certbot (SSL)
- Zoho Mail + Resend (email infrastructure)

## Lessons Learned
- DNS scanners miss subdomains not explicitly listed - always audit manually
- Grey vs orange cloud distinction is critical for API backends
- Nameserver migration order matters: add all records first, change nameservers last
